---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const base = `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<BaseLayout title="Japan with Our Child: 11-Day Itinerary | Travel With Masala Chai" description="Our 11-day family itinerary through Tokyo, Mt. Fuji and Hokkaido.">
  <article class="post">
    <header>
      <a href={`${base}journal/japan/`}>← Back to Japan journal</a>
      <p>JAPAN · FAMILY TRAVEL</p>
      <h1>Japan with our Child: Our 11-Day Japan Itinerary</h1>
      <div class="route">Tokyo → Kawaguchiko → Tokyo → Sapporo → Asahikawa → Biei &amp; Furano → Noboribetsu → Sapporo → Otaru → Tokyo/Narita</div>
    </header>
    <div class="content">
      <p class="lead">Japan had been on our travel list for a while, but travelling around Japan with a young child meant we wanted to balance sightseeing with enough time to slow down.</p>
      <p>Instead of trying the usual “Golden Triangle” of Tokyo, Kyoto and Osaka, we planned our trip around three main areas: <strong>Tokyo, the Mt. Fuji region and Hokkaido</strong>.</p>
      <p>Our route took us from Tokyo to Kawaguchiko, then back to Tokyo before flying north to Sapporo. From Sapporo, we explored several parts of Hokkaido through day trips and short stays, discovering often untouched parts of Japan.</p>
      <figure class="wide"><img src={`${base}images/japan/teamlab-family.jpg`} alt="Family moment at teamLab in Tokyo" /><figcaption>Our first evening in Tokyo included TeamLab.</figcaption></figure>
      <h2>Days 1–4: Tokyo &amp; Mt. Fuji</h2>
      <p>We flew ANA from Mumbai and landed in Tokyo. We started in Tokyo after arriving at Narita Airport. Our first evening was kept relatively simple, with a visit to TeamLab and some time around Nakamise Shopping Street.</p>
      <p>The following morning, we travelled towards Kawaguchiko, our base for exploring the Mt. Fuji area. Over the next few days, we visited Lake Kawaguchiko, Oishi Park, Chureito Pagoda, Oshino Hakkai, Fujisan Station, Kawaguchiko lakeshore and Fuji Safari Park.</p>
      <div class="photo-grid"><img src={`${base}images/japan/lake-kawaguchiko-mount-fuji.jpg`} alt="Mount Fuji from Lake Kawaguchiko" /><img src={`${base}images/japan/fuji-safari-wallaby.jpg`} alt="Child meeting a wallaby at Fuji Safari Park" /><img src={`${base}images/japan/oshino-hakkai-mount-fuji.jpg`} alt="Oshino Hakkai with Mount Fuji" /></div>
      <h2>Days 5–10: Hokkaido</h2>
      <p>From Tokyo, we flew to New Chitose Airport and based ourselves mainly around Sapporo. Hokkaido was a completely different experience from Tokyo.</p>
      <p>Instead of one large city itinerary, we used Sapporo as a base for several day trips and stayed overnight in Furano and Noboribetsu so we did not have to drive back to Sapporo and tire ourselves.</p>
      <p>Our plans included Sapporo, Asahikawa, Asahiyama Zoo, Biei, Furano, Noboribetsu and Otaru.</p>
      <figure class="wide"><img src={`${base}images/japan/biei-blue-pond.jpg`} alt="Blue Pond in Biei" /><figcaption>The brilliant blue waters of Biei.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="photo-grid"><img src={`${base}images/japan/furano-flower-fields.jpg`} alt="Flower fields in Furano" /><img src={`${base}images/japan/noboribetsu-hell-valley.jpg`} alt="Noboribetsu Hell Valley at dusk" /><img src={`${base}images/japan/otaru-canal.jpg`} alt="Boat on Otaru Canal" /></div>
      <h2>Travelling with a child</h2>
      <p>The biggest lesson from planning Japan with a child was not to make every day about sightseeing. Some days involved long train journeys. Others were deliberately slower.</p>
      <p>We kept snacks with us, planned breaks and tried to avoid changing hotels every night. For the Mt. Fuji section especially, staying for two nights gave us more flexibility because weather can affect whether Mt. Fuji is actually visible.</p>
      <h2>What we liked about this itinerary</h2>
      <p>The biggest advantage of this route was the variety. Tokyo gave us the big-city experience. Kawaguchiko gave us Mt. Fuji and slower lakeside days. Hokkaido brought a completely different landscape, with wildlife, countryside, small towns and onsen experiences.</p>
      <p>It was a lot of travel, but it also gave us very different sides of Japan in one trip.</p>
      <figure class="wide"><img src={`${base}images/japan/otaru-canal-clock.jpg`} alt="Otaru Canal beneath a bright autumn sky" /></figure>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

